asset:        filmagic-keyframes.png
title:        README gallery: keyframe value/velocity graphs
author:       Filmagic contributors (screenshot); film/footage/music credits below
source:       original work (screenshot of Filmagic captured with its ui.screenshot control command)
license:      MIT OR Apache-2.0 (screenshot); embedded film frames are public domain, music CC0-1.0
license-file:
added:        2026-11-12 by @vansyson1308
notes:        Editing workspace at 1600x1000 points (1.5x, 2400x1500 px): Effect Controls of the title shot of the Trailer sequence with the eased Motion > Scale push-in (ease-out and ease-in keyframes) and its value and velocity graphs; the Program monitor on the Night of the Living Dead (1968) title card over the country road; the Properties panel (Transform, Crop); Project panel; Timeline; Audio Meters with loudness readings. All UI icons are drawn in code; fonts are Inter and JetBrains Mono (OFL-1.1). Contains no Adobe artwork. Rebuild and recapture with the scripts in docs/images/scripts/ (README there): build_trailer.py, then capture_gallery.py keyframes.
Media visible or used in the screenshot (none of it is committed to the repository):
  - Night of the Living Dead (1968), dir. George A. Romero, Image Ten. US public domain (released without a copyright notice). Source: https://archive.org/details/night-of-the-living-dead-1968-english (Public Domain Mark).
  - Carnival of Souls (1962), dir. Herk Harvey, Harcourt Productions. US public domain. Source: https://archive.org/details/CarnivalOfSoulsVideoQualityUpgrade (Public Domain Mark).
  - Charade (1963), dir. Stanley Donen, Universal. US public domain (defective copyright notice). Source: https://archive.org/details/charade-1963-cary-grant-audrey-hepburn-comedy-mystery-romance-thriller-full-movie (Public Domain Mark).
  - Earth Views from the ISS (NASA, NHQ_2020_1221, no-music edition). US Government work, public domain; NASA does not endorse this project. Source: https://images.nasa.gov/details/NHQ_2020_1221_Earth%20Views
  - Chopin, Nocturne Op. 48 No. 1 and Ballade No. 1, performed for Musopen. CC0-1.0. Source: https://archive.org/details/musopen-chopin (shown only as waveforms).
